+4.1 For the mesh-family branch, set $A_N=D\mathcal K_N(z_N^0)$ and $T_N(z)=z-A_N^{-1}\mathcal K_N(z)$. If $\varepsilon_N=0$, $z_N^0$ is already a root. Otherwise take the closed ball of radius $R_N=2\beta\varepsilon_N$. The residual bound gives $\|T_N(z_N^0)-z_N^0\|\le\beta\varepsilon_N=R_N/2$. For $z$ in that ball, the uniform Lipschitz bound gives $\|DT_N(z)\|=\|I-A_N^{-1}D\mathcal K_N(z)\|\le\beta L R_N\le1/2$. Thus $T_N$ maps the ball into itself and is a contraction. Completeness [F7] gives its unique fixed point $z_N^*$, which is exactly the unique root of $\mathcal K_N$ there and satisfies $\|z_N^*-z_N^0\|\le R_N$. [A1, F7, algebra]
+
